1
00:00:01,105 --> 00:00:03,023
- Previously on "The Ark."

2
00:00:03,024 --> 00:00:04,190
- That doctor said something
about me being the one.

3
00:00:04,191 --> 00:00:05,525
What the hell
is Drake doing to them?

4
00:00:05,526 --> 00:00:06,901
- It's like
some kind of puzzle.

5
00:00:06,902 --> 00:00:08,278
That feels like progress.

6
00:00:08,279 --> 00:00:11,322
- I had to go to freaking space
to get away from him.

7
00:00:11,323 --> 00:00:13,491
- Gen-3 brain cells
are unstable,

8
00:00:13,492 --> 00:00:15,952
and they do develop psychosis.

9
00:00:15,953 --> 00:00:17,328
[explosion booming]

10
00:00:17,329 --> 00:00:18,955
- Generators don't just
explode without reason.

11
00:00:18,956 --> 00:00:20,165
- What are you implying?

12
00:00:20,166 --> 00:00:21,041
- This is what
Angus was wearing

13
00:00:21,042 --> 00:00:22,375
the last time I saw him.

14
00:00:22,376 --> 00:00:24,044
- The "Ark 1" shuttle is gone.

15
00:00:24,045 --> 00:00:25,170
- Find her.

16
00:00:25,171 --> 00:00:26,296
- But I'm not Gen-3.

17
00:00:26,297 --> 00:00:28,548
- Yes, darling, you are.

18
00:00:28,549 --> 00:00:29,967
[dramatic music]

19
00:00:34,597 --> 00:00:36,056
[laughter]

20
00:00:36,057 --> 00:00:37,224
- She don't look too good.

21
00:00:38,893 --> 00:00:40,226
- You all right, sis?

22
00:00:40,227 --> 00:00:42,020
- Let's just say
I'm having a difficult time

23
00:00:42,021 --> 00:00:43,730
believing I'm a Gen-3 clone.

24
00:00:43,731 --> 00:00:45,398
- Mm.

25
00:00:45,399 --> 00:00:46,775
- What can I say, darling?

26
00:00:46,776 --> 00:00:48,068
You are.

27
00:00:48,069 --> 00:00:49,861
- Can you explain
how that can be?

28
00:00:49,862 --> 00:00:51,071
- It just--it doesn't track.

29
00:00:51,072 --> 00:00:52,238
She never went psychotic.

30
00:00:52,239 --> 00:00:53,615
- Which is exactly why

31
00:00:53,616 --> 00:00:55,367
Kurtis has been
looking for you.

32
00:00:55,368 --> 00:00:57,368
He wants to use you
to find a way

33
00:00:57,369 --> 00:00:59,370
to stop the Gen-3s
from ever turning.

34
00:00:59,371 --> 00:01:01,748
- If that's the case,
why didn't Drake just tell her?

35
00:01:01,749 --> 00:01:03,166
- I'm guessing
he didn't think I'd be open

36
00:01:03,167 --> 00:01:04,918
to impromptu brain surgery.

37
00:01:04,919 --> 00:01:08,004
- Alls I know is he thinks
something inside of you

38
00:01:08,005 --> 00:01:09,589
is the answer
to all his problems.

39
00:01:09,590 --> 00:01:11,633
- You mean the problems
with the Gen-3s?

40
00:01:11,634 --> 00:01:12,842
- Mm-mm.

41
00:01:12,843 --> 00:01:15,178
That is not how he said it.

42
00:01:15,179 --> 00:01:17,430
He specifically said,
"If I could just find

43
00:01:17,431 --> 00:01:20,100
the right one, it would
solve all my problems."

44
00:01:20,101 --> 00:01:21,267
- Mm.

45
00:01:21,268 --> 00:01:24,229
[tense music]

46
00:01:24,230 --> 00:01:27,148
♪ ♪

47
00:01:27,149 --> 00:01:29,317
- Former EF and GSA personnel

48
00:01:29,318 --> 00:01:32,195
are blaming each other
for the generator explosions.

49
00:01:32,196 --> 00:01:34,072
We need to give them
the information--

50
00:01:34,073 --> 00:01:35,990
- I'm afraid
he still hasn't woken,

51
00:01:35,991 --> 00:01:37,492
so we know nothing about him.

52
00:01:37,493 --> 00:01:40,203
- I've checked our manifests.
He isn't "EF-2."

53
00:01:40,204 --> 00:01:41,579
- Are you certain?

54
00:01:41,580 --> 00:01:42,747
- Absolutely.

55
00:01:42,748 --> 00:01:45,125
- Well, he doesn't appear
on any Ark manifests.

56
00:01:45,126 --> 00:01:46,376
- You must have
missed something.

57
00:01:46,377 --> 00:01:48,920
GSA manifests are solid.

58
00:01:48,921 --> 00:01:50,463
- As are EFs'.

59
00:01:50,464 --> 00:01:52,674
- All right, so he's not
on either manifest.

60
00:01:52,675 --> 00:01:55,510
The truth is he could be
a stowaway from either ship.

61
00:01:55,511 --> 00:01:58,722
- But if he's been
hiding out all this time,

62
00:01:58,723 --> 00:02:02,183
why expose himself now
to sabotage the generator?

63
00:02:02,184 --> 00:02:03,393
- I don't know.

64
00:02:03,394 --> 00:02:04,602
But as soon as
he regains consciousness,

65
00:02:04,603 --> 00:02:06,021
we'll interrogate him
to find out.

66
00:02:06,022 --> 00:02:07,647
- Any idea when that might be?

67
00:02:07,648 --> 00:02:10,191
- [sighs] Well, I don't
even know if he'll wake up,

68
00:02:10,192 --> 00:02:11,818
much less when.

69
00:02:11,819 --> 00:02:15,655
- More important question is
are there more like him?

70
00:02:15,656 --> 00:02:16,990
[dramatic music]

71
00:02:16,991 --> 00:02:20,702
Saboteurs hiding among us.

72
00:02:20,703 --> 00:02:23,580
[uplifting music]

73
00:02:23,581 --> 00:02:30,504
♪ ♪

74
00:02:40,723 --> 00:02:43,642
[metallic clanking]

75
00:03:00,451 --> 00:03:03,119
- Glad you made it back safely.

76
00:03:03,120 --> 00:03:05,288
- So sorry to hear about Brice.

77
00:03:05,289 --> 00:03:06,956
He had my respect.

78
00:03:06,957 --> 00:03:08,083
- Thank you.

79
00:03:08,084 --> 00:03:09,626
And I'm sorry
about Doc Morozov.

80
00:03:09,627 --> 00:03:11,795
She was a great addition
to "Ark 1."

81
00:03:11,796 --> 00:03:14,005
Jill and Clark, the two clones
from Drake's ship,

82
00:03:14,006 --> 00:03:15,131
they're on the next shuttle.

83
00:03:15,132 --> 00:03:16,841
Can we find space for them?

84
00:03:16,842 --> 00:03:18,301
- Are they a security risk?

85
00:03:18,302 --> 00:03:20,428
- Uh, no, I don't think so.

86
00:03:20,429 --> 00:03:24,391
- Then of course.
Consider it done.

87
00:03:24,392 --> 00:03:25,642
- Any word on "EF-1"?

88
00:03:25,643 --> 00:03:28,353
- None.
Tried everything.

89
00:03:28,354 --> 00:03:30,855
No contact.

90
00:03:30,856 --> 00:03:31,856
- I'm sorry.

91
00:03:31,857 --> 00:03:34,275
- Captain.

92
00:03:34,276 --> 00:03:37,320
[somber music]

93
00:03:37,321 --> 00:03:39,364
- Excuse me.

94
00:03:39,365 --> 00:03:45,912
♪ ♪

95
00:03:45,913 --> 00:03:47,205
Eva.

96
00:03:47,206 --> 00:03:48,873
♪ ♪

97
00:03:48,874 --> 00:03:50,250
- Strickland.

98
00:03:50,251 --> 00:03:51,292
Have you found Angus?

99
00:03:51,293 --> 00:03:52,502
- No. I'm so sorry.

100
00:03:52,503 --> 00:03:53,753
We're still looking.

101
00:03:53,754 --> 00:03:54,796
- OK.

102
00:03:54,797 --> 00:03:57,090
Um, what can I do?
I want to help.

103
00:03:57,091 --> 00:03:59,300
- Come with me.
I'll fill you in.

104
00:03:59,301 --> 00:04:05,306
♪ ♪

105
00:04:05,307 --> 00:04:08,935
[indistinct chatter]

106
00:04:08,936 --> 00:04:11,312
- Not too shabby, eh?

107
00:04:11,313 --> 00:04:12,981
- Yeah.

108
00:04:12,982 --> 00:04:16,861
I know you said it was
beautiful, but wow.

109
00:04:20,781 --> 00:04:22,782
Who is that?

110
00:04:22,783 --> 00:04:24,409
- Oh, that's Ves.

111
00:04:24,410 --> 00:04:26,411
Yeah, she's one of
the council members.

112
00:04:26,412 --> 00:04:29,414
I think she came here
on "Ark 11."

113
00:04:29,415 --> 00:04:32,917
[soft dramatic music]

114
00:04:32,918 --> 00:04:34,461
- We can't find
any physical tracks,

115
00:04:34,462 --> 00:04:35,628
no sign of a struggle.

116
00:04:35,629 --> 00:04:36,963
The only thing
we came across was

117
00:04:36,964 --> 00:04:38,506
an abnormality
in the vegetation.

118
00:04:38,507 --> 00:04:39,841
- Well, what kind
of abnormality?

119
00:04:39,842 --> 00:04:40,925
- It was strange.

120
00:04:40,926 --> 00:04:43,386
A perfect circle of dead,
burnt vegetation.

121
00:04:43,387 --> 00:04:44,846
We tested it to see
if there were any hints

122
00:04:44,847 --> 00:04:46,514
of Kelly's or Angus's
whereabouts,

123
00:04:46,515 --> 00:04:47,974
but there doesn't seem
to be a connection.

124
00:04:47,975 --> 00:04:49,517
- Well, have you tried
tracing their WDs?

125
00:04:49,518 --> 00:04:50,685
- Yes. Nothing.

126
00:04:50,686 --> 00:04:53,146
No emergency beacon,
no signal at all.

127
00:04:53,147 --> 00:04:55,315
- OK, well,

128
00:04:55,316 --> 00:04:56,649
now that "Ark 1" is here,
we may be able

129
00:04:56,650 --> 00:04:58,443
to tap into the array receiver
from the aft section.

130
00:04:58,444 --> 00:04:59,652
- To do what?

131
00:04:59,653 --> 00:05:00,904
- It's more sensitive
than the receivers

132
00:05:00,905 --> 00:05:02,781
we use here to communicate
on Homebase,

133
00:05:02,782 --> 00:05:05,200
so we may be able to pick up
Angus and Kelly's WDs

134
00:05:05,201 --> 00:05:06,659
even if the signal's weak.

135
00:05:06,660 --> 00:05:09,370
- Get started and let me know
if you need anything.

136
00:05:09,371 --> 00:05:12,540
Oh, and Alicia.

137
00:05:12,541 --> 00:05:16,795
I really miss
having you around.

138
00:05:16,796 --> 00:05:18,005
Go.

139
00:05:19,173 --> 00:05:22,259
[EKG beeping steadily]

140
00:05:24,929 --> 00:05:26,262
- Sharon.

141
00:05:26,263 --> 00:05:27,598
Oh, you're back. I'm--

142
00:05:29,016 --> 00:05:31,226
I'm--I'm so sorry I couldn't
meet you at the shuttle.

143
00:05:31,227 --> 00:05:33,186
It's been a crazy morning.

144
00:05:33,187 --> 00:05:34,312
- Have you known all this time?

145
00:05:34,313 --> 00:05:35,689
- Known what?

146
00:05:38,025 --> 00:05:39,734
- That I'm Gen-3.

147
00:05:39,735 --> 00:05:42,320
[soft dramatic music]

148
00:05:42,321 --> 00:05:45,990
- Uh...

149
00:05:45,991 --> 00:05:47,283
yes.

150
00:05:47,284 --> 00:05:50,370
♪ ♪

151
00:05:50,371 --> 00:05:52,664
- Sanji.

152
00:05:52,665 --> 00:05:55,083
- But Dr. Hall
swore me to secrecy.

153
00:05:55,084 --> 00:05:57,085
- Why?

154
00:05:57,086 --> 00:05:59,671
- In addition
to the officially sanctioned

155
00:05:59,672 --> 00:06:02,048
experiments that
Hall conducted on you,

156
00:06:02,049 --> 00:06:05,218
he was also doing

157
00:06:05,219 --> 00:06:08,304
unsanctioned ones to solve
the Gen-3 brain anomaly.

158
00:06:08,305 --> 00:06:11,141
- Yeah, to stop them--
us from ever turning psychotic.

159
00:06:11,142 --> 00:06:12,308
I know.

160
00:06:12,309 --> 00:06:13,518
- Yeah, eventually,
one of the things

161
00:06:13,519 --> 00:06:15,895
he tried on you
actually worked,

162
00:06:15,896 --> 00:06:18,690
which is why
you haven't turned.

163
00:06:18,691 --> 00:06:20,483
- If what you're saying
is true,

164
00:06:20,484 --> 00:06:22,527
then why didn't he give it
to all the Gen-3s?

165
00:06:22,528 --> 00:06:25,113
- Because he couldn't
reproduce the experiment.

166
00:06:25,114 --> 00:06:27,574
He couldn't get it
to work on your sister.

167
00:06:27,575 --> 00:06:30,076
Or--or any of the other Gen-3s.

168
00:06:30,077 --> 00:06:33,037
[somber music]

169
00:06:33,038 --> 00:06:37,167
♪ ♪

170
00:06:37,168 --> 00:06:38,877
- Denise was Gen-3?

171
00:06:38,878 --> 00:06:40,378
- Uh...

172
00:06:40,379 --> 00:06:41,880
- He lied to us.

173
00:06:41,881 --> 00:06:44,007
He said my sister and I
were Gen-2s.

174
00:06:44,008 --> 00:06:46,468
- He swore me to secrecy when
he brought you on "Ark 1."

175
00:06:46,469 --> 00:06:47,594
I couldn't tell you.

176
00:06:47,595 --> 00:06:50,180
- But why?
- T-to protect you.

177
00:06:50,181 --> 00:06:52,849
He didn't want other people
to find out you were Gen-3

178
00:06:52,850 --> 00:06:55,393
and start experimenting on you.

179
00:06:55,394 --> 00:06:57,187
- Like Drake tried to.

180
00:06:57,188 --> 00:06:58,688
And he must have known somehow.

181
00:06:58,689 --> 00:07:01,775
And it might help
if I knew about this, Sanji,

182
00:07:01,776 --> 00:07:04,027
before I went to his ship.

183
00:07:04,028 --> 00:07:08,156
♪ ♪

184
00:07:08,157 --> 00:07:11,118
[birds singing]

185
00:07:12,161 --> 00:07:15,039
[indistinct chatter]

186
00:07:18,751 --> 00:07:21,669
- Thought I might
find you here.

187
00:07:21,670 --> 00:07:23,381
I'll have what she's having.

188
00:07:25,299 --> 00:07:27,217
So come on.

189
00:07:27,218 --> 00:07:29,177
Out with it.

190
00:07:29,178 --> 00:07:30,762
- How would you feel
if you just found out

191
00:07:30,763 --> 00:07:32,472
you were the only Gen-3
in the universe

192
00:07:32,473 --> 00:07:34,349
who's not psychotic?

193
00:07:34,350 --> 00:07:35,850
- Mm.

194
00:07:35,851 --> 00:07:37,769
Well, probably better than
the ones who are psychotic.

195
00:07:37,770 --> 00:07:41,022
Thanks.

196
00:07:41,023 --> 00:07:44,109
- Feel like I don't
know who I am anymore.

197
00:07:44,110 --> 00:07:46,945
- Oh, come on.
You're kidding, right?

198
00:07:46,946 --> 00:07:50,740
Gen-2, Gen-3,
Gen-1,000, doesn't matter.

199
00:07:50,741 --> 00:07:53,660
You're still Sharon Garnet.

200
00:07:53,661 --> 00:07:55,787
- Whatever Dr. Hall
did to me on Earth,

201
00:07:55,788 --> 00:08:00,291
I survived and Denise didn't.

202
00:08:00,292 --> 00:08:05,171
And it feels wrong.

203
00:08:05,172 --> 00:08:06,256
- Mm.

204
00:08:06,257 --> 00:08:09,300
The old survivor's guilt, eh?

205
00:08:09,301 --> 00:08:10,593
Well, lucky for you,

206
00:08:10,594 --> 00:08:13,263
I've got some experience
in that department too.

207
00:08:13,264 --> 00:08:16,391
I grew up in a lab full of
clones all designed to die,

208
00:08:16,392 --> 00:08:18,018
and yet, here I am.

209
00:08:21,021 --> 00:08:24,190
Look, the only way
to move forwards

210
00:08:24,191 --> 00:08:28,319
is to keep on doing
the good that you do.

211
00:08:28,320 --> 00:08:31,615
Least that way, it doesn't feel
like it was all for nothing.

212
00:08:33,701 --> 00:08:35,577
Curly.
- [chuckles]

213
00:08:35,578 --> 00:08:38,496
- Look at me.

214
00:08:38,497 --> 00:08:42,333
To keeping on keeping on?

215
00:08:42,334 --> 00:08:44,586
- To keeping on keeping on.

216
00:08:44,587 --> 00:08:46,796
- You asked for us?

217
00:08:46,797 --> 00:08:48,590
- Is Sheriff Petrovic awake?

218
00:08:48,591 --> 00:08:49,841
- I'm afraid not.

219
00:08:49,842 --> 00:08:50,884
- What about our mystery man?

220
00:08:50,885 --> 00:08:52,635
- No, but we have
new information

221
00:08:52,636 --> 00:08:54,304
I thought the council
should know.

222
00:08:54,305 --> 00:08:57,223
There's no change in
our John Doe's condition,

223
00:08:57,224 --> 00:09:00,185
but we found something
you should see.

224
00:09:00,186 --> 00:09:02,645
- This is an MRI scan
of his head.

225
00:09:02,646 --> 00:09:04,022
- Yeah--if you look closely,

226
00:09:04,023 --> 00:09:07,567
you'll see he has three sets
of cochleas in each ear.

227
00:09:07,568 --> 00:09:09,277
- We all have one per ear.

228
00:09:09,278 --> 00:09:12,364
- His vocal cords also
have noticeable differences

229
00:09:12,365 --> 00:09:13,656
from ours.

230
00:09:13,657 --> 00:09:15,408
- Was there something
that was done to him?

231
00:09:15,409 --> 00:09:16,993
- There are no signs of surgery

232
00:09:16,994 --> 00:09:19,079
or any other
medical interventions.

233
00:09:19,080 --> 00:09:21,664
Far as I can tell,
he was born this way.

234
00:09:21,665 --> 00:09:23,708
- Could he be a humanoid
who evolved here

235
00:09:23,709 --> 00:09:25,418
the same way we did on Earth,

236
00:09:25,419 --> 00:09:27,087
just with these
few differences?

237
00:09:27,088 --> 00:09:29,214
- I don't know.
That's unlikely.

238
00:09:29,215 --> 00:09:31,424
- I was on the first Ark
that arrived here.

239
00:09:31,425 --> 00:09:33,802
We swept the area multiple
times for sign of life.

240
00:09:33,803 --> 00:09:36,596
Nothing but plants and birds.

241
00:09:36,597 --> 00:09:38,431
[soft suspenseful music]

242
00:09:38,432 --> 00:09:40,225
- So who is he?

243
00:09:40,226 --> 00:09:44,938
- A better question might be
what is he?

244
00:09:44,939 --> 00:09:48,192
[suspenseful music]

245
00:09:51,404 --> 00:09:52,405
[birds singing]

246
00:09:58,202 --> 00:10:01,663
- Eva, I've been
looking for you.

247
00:10:01,664 --> 00:10:03,415
Why aren't you out
fixing the generators?

248
00:10:03,416 --> 00:10:04,416
- I am.

249
00:10:04,417 --> 00:10:06,126
Oh.

250
00:10:06,127 --> 00:10:07,335
I mean I will.

251
00:10:07,336 --> 00:10:08,920
- What are you doing?

252
00:10:08,921 --> 00:10:12,340
- OK, have to listen to this.

253
00:10:12,341 --> 00:10:15,677
I triangulated a signal.

254
00:10:15,678 --> 00:10:17,679
It's coming from Ross.

255
00:10:17,680 --> 00:10:18,722
- Ross?

256
00:10:18,723 --> 00:10:19,723
- Yes.

257
00:10:19,724 --> 00:10:21,182
I think it's James.

258
00:10:21,183 --> 00:10:22,684
Here. Listen.

259
00:10:22,685 --> 00:10:24,978
[beeping]

260
00:10:24,979 --> 00:10:26,855
[crackling electronic droning]
- [sighs] I don't--

261
00:10:26,856 --> 00:10:30,024
[electronic chime]
- Just--OK, just listen again.

262
00:10:30,025 --> 00:10:31,693
OK? There.

263
00:10:31,694 --> 00:10:32,610
[electronic chime]

264
00:10:32,611 --> 00:10:34,279
[crackling electronic droning]

265
00:10:34,280 --> 00:10:36,531
[electronic chime]
There. At the end.

266
00:10:36,532 --> 00:10:37,574
Hear it?
[electronic chime]

267
00:10:37,575 --> 00:10:39,284
It's an unnatural modulation.

268
00:10:39,285 --> 00:10:41,244
- That could be any kind
of interference.

269
00:10:41,245 --> 00:10:45,206
A solar flare,
another radio signal.

270
00:10:45,207 --> 00:10:47,250
Eva, this isn't healthy.

271
00:10:47,251 --> 00:10:49,294
You have to let this go.

272
00:10:49,295 --> 00:10:50,670
- Yeah, but what if
he's trying to--

273
00:10:50,671 --> 00:10:52,881
- Eva.

274
00:10:52,882 --> 00:10:55,717
James is gone.

275
00:10:55,718 --> 00:10:59,472
I saw him die.

276
00:11:00,973 --> 00:11:03,892
- But it feels wrong
to think about anything else.

277
00:11:03,893 --> 00:11:05,185
- I know.

278
00:11:05,186 --> 00:11:06,352
OK?

279
00:11:06,353 --> 00:11:08,730
But the temperatures
are rising,

280
00:11:08,731 --> 00:11:11,566
and we have a whole section
of the colony without power

281
00:11:11,567 --> 00:11:14,069
and we need you.

282
00:11:14,070 --> 00:11:16,362
[soft dramatic music]

283
00:11:16,363 --> 00:11:18,823
So do you think you could go
and fix some generators?

284
00:11:18,824 --> 00:11:21,534
♪ ♪

285
00:11:21,535 --> 00:11:22,660
- OK.

286
00:11:22,661 --> 00:11:26,831
♪ ♪

287
00:11:26,832 --> 00:11:28,917
- I'll let you have
some privacy.

288
00:11:28,918 --> 00:11:34,756
♪ ♪

289
00:11:34,757 --> 00:11:36,257
[door closes]

290
00:11:36,258 --> 00:11:39,260
[suspenseful music]

291
00:11:39,261 --> 00:11:46,267
♪ ♪

292
00:11:46,268 --> 00:11:49,979
[EKG beeping steadily]

293
00:11:49,980 --> 00:11:52,941
[tense music]

294
00:11:52,942 --> 00:11:57,112
♪ ♪

295
00:11:57,113 --> 00:11:59,364
[EKG beeping quickly]

296
00:11:59,365 --> 00:12:02,242
- Who are you?

297
00:12:02,243 --> 00:12:03,702
What do you want?

298
00:12:03,703 --> 00:12:05,078
- What's going on?

299
00:12:05,079 --> 00:12:06,788
- I don't know.
He just woke up.

300
00:12:06,789 --> 00:12:08,164
I came to see if he's OK.

301
00:12:08,165 --> 00:12:09,708
- OK, thank you.
I can take it from here.

302
00:12:09,709 --> 00:12:12,127
My name is Dr. Kabir.
You're safe.

303
00:12:12,128 --> 00:12:14,462
Can you tell me who you are?

304
00:12:14,463 --> 00:12:15,922
Why is he so afraid of you?

305
00:12:15,923 --> 00:12:17,924
- I-I don't know.

306
00:12:17,925 --> 00:12:19,175
I should get back to work.

307
00:12:19,176 --> 00:12:20,927
Let us know
if he says anything.

308
00:12:20,928 --> 00:12:22,095
- OK. Thank you.

309
00:12:22,096 --> 00:12:24,264
Hey, stay with me.
Stay with me.

310
00:12:24,265 --> 00:12:27,726
You're OK. You're OK.
You're OK.

311
00:12:27,727 --> 00:12:28,852
You're OK.

312
00:12:28,853 --> 00:12:31,897
[birds singing]

313
00:12:42,241 --> 00:12:43,992
- No one's going to rescue us.

314
00:12:43,993 --> 00:12:45,326
You know that, right?

315
00:12:45,327 --> 00:12:47,412
- Why do you say that?

316
00:12:47,413 --> 00:12:48,955
- They probably think
I killed you

317
00:12:48,956 --> 00:12:51,249
and gave up the search.

318
00:12:51,250 --> 00:12:53,793
Isn't that what
you would think?

319
00:12:53,794 --> 00:12:56,129
- Alicia won't let them
give up.

320
00:12:56,130 --> 00:12:58,757
- You really love her,
don't you?

321
00:12:58,758 --> 00:13:00,300
- That doesn't make you mad,
does it?

322
00:13:00,301 --> 00:13:01,968
- No.

323
00:13:01,969 --> 00:13:03,803
I'm so over you.
- Oh.

324
00:13:03,804 --> 00:13:06,264
- I don't even know why
I was ever attracted to you.

325
00:13:06,265 --> 00:13:07,265
- Thanks.

326
00:13:07,266 --> 00:13:09,726
Thank you, I guess.

327
00:13:09,727 --> 00:13:11,561
[sniffles]

328
00:13:11,562 --> 00:13:13,438
- So is Alicia as into you
as you're into her?

329
00:13:13,439 --> 00:13:15,148
- Where is this going, Kelly?

330
00:13:15,149 --> 00:13:16,524
- Just trying
to gauge the odds

331
00:13:16,525 --> 00:13:19,652
of the Homebase people
coming for us or not.

332
00:13:19,653 --> 00:13:22,155
- She won't let them quit.

333
00:13:22,156 --> 00:13:24,532
- Assuming she even knows.

334
00:13:24,533 --> 00:13:27,202
She's a million light years
away on the Ark, remember?

335
00:13:27,203 --> 00:13:29,704
[soft somber music]

336
00:13:29,705 --> 00:13:32,123
- Damn.

337
00:13:32,124 --> 00:13:34,250
I didn't even think of that.

338
00:13:34,251 --> 00:13:37,380
[mechanical whirring]

339
00:13:41,342 --> 00:13:42,760
[electricity crackling]

340
00:13:48,599 --> 00:13:51,643
- Fuel levels are adequate
and the generator is grounded.

341
00:13:51,644 --> 00:13:53,186
Did you turn on the fuel valve?

342
00:13:53,187 --> 00:13:54,562
- Do you know,

343
00:13:54,563 --> 00:13:56,231
I can't believe you think
so little of me, Markovic.

344
00:13:56,232 --> 00:13:58,942
- OK, I'll take that as a yes.

345
00:13:58,943 --> 00:13:59,984
Powering on?

346
00:13:59,985 --> 00:14:01,236
- Let's do it.

347
00:14:01,237 --> 00:14:02,362
[electricity thrumming]

348
00:14:02,363 --> 00:14:05,031
[chuckles] Yeah.

349
00:14:05,032 --> 00:14:06,784
Come on.

350
00:14:09,412 --> 00:14:11,496
- I'm Captain Sharon Garnet.

351
00:14:11,497 --> 00:14:12,997
Can you understand me?

352
00:14:12,998 --> 00:14:14,332
[electricity thrumming]

353
00:14:14,333 --> 00:14:16,167
Huh. Looks like Eva got one
of the generators working.

354
00:14:16,168 --> 00:14:18,002
- [groaning]

355
00:14:18,003 --> 00:14:19,212
- What's wrong with him?

356
00:14:19,213 --> 00:14:20,463
- I don't know.
- [screaming]

357
00:14:20,464 --> 00:14:23,466
- Are you OK? Talk to me.
- [groaning]

358
00:14:23,467 --> 00:14:25,051
- Maybe it's the lights?

359
00:14:25,052 --> 00:14:27,595
- [screaming]

360
00:14:27,596 --> 00:14:29,472
We're hurting him somehow! I--

361
00:14:29,473 --> 00:14:32,517
[tense music]

362
00:14:32,518 --> 00:14:33,936
♪ ♪

363
00:14:38,566 --> 00:14:40,442
[soft suspenseful music]

364
00:14:40,443 --> 00:14:42,277
- Something to do
with the generator sound

365
00:14:42,278 --> 00:14:44,404
was hurting his ears?

366
00:14:44,405 --> 00:14:45,947
- Well, the extra cochlea
probably allow him

367
00:14:45,948 --> 00:14:48,033
to hear frequencies
our ears can't.

368
00:14:48,034 --> 00:14:50,034
Uh, the generators must emit

369
00:14:50,035 --> 00:14:52,412
some kind of piercing,
high frequency sound?

370
00:14:52,413 --> 00:14:54,205
- At least now we know
why he blew them up.

371
00:14:54,206 --> 00:14:58,044
♪ ♪

372
00:15:01,797 --> 00:15:04,215
- Good to see you
back in action, Eva.

373
00:15:04,216 --> 00:15:06,092
- Thanks.

374
00:15:06,093 --> 00:15:08,595
I just miss him.

375
00:15:08,596 --> 00:15:11,473
- If you ever need
a shoulder to cry on,

376
00:15:11,474 --> 00:15:13,141
I've got a spare one.

377
00:15:13,142 --> 00:15:14,559
- Thank you.

378
00:15:14,560 --> 00:15:16,519
- Aye, might not be quite
as big and as strapping

379
00:15:16,520 --> 00:15:20,732
as Brice's was,
but it'll do in a pinch.

380
00:15:20,733 --> 00:15:24,944
- I just have to make myself
accept that he's gone.

381
00:15:24,945 --> 00:15:27,739
[somber music]

382
00:15:27,740 --> 00:15:30,158
[electricity crackling]

383
00:15:30,159 --> 00:15:32,744
- [panting]

384
00:15:32,745 --> 00:15:34,914
[mechanical whirring]

385
00:15:37,666 --> 00:15:39,709
[metallic rattling]

386
00:15:39,710 --> 00:15:41,669
[dramatic music]

387
00:15:41,670 --> 00:15:44,589
- Who's there?

388
00:15:44,590 --> 00:15:46,549
- Lieutenant Brice?

389
00:15:46,550 --> 00:15:50,011
It's me, Dr. Morozov.

390
00:15:50,012 --> 00:15:51,846
- Where am I?

391
00:15:51,847 --> 00:15:54,891
- We're still on Ross.

392
00:15:54,892 --> 00:15:56,309
- How?

393
00:15:56,310 --> 00:15:58,687
- I dragged you away
from those creatures outside.

394
00:15:58,688 --> 00:16:04,776
I did my best to patch you up
with what I could find.

395
00:16:04,777 --> 00:16:07,904
You've been out
for a long time.

396
00:16:07,905 --> 00:16:10,990
- We, uh, we have to get
a signal to "Ark 1"

397
00:16:10,991 --> 00:16:12,367
before it leaves orbit.

398
00:16:12,368 --> 00:16:15,995
- I hate to tell you,
but "Ark 1" left days ago.

399
00:16:15,996 --> 00:16:18,873
[tense music]

400
00:16:18,874 --> 00:16:20,875
♪ ♪

401
00:16:20,876 --> 00:16:23,254
[electricity crackling]

402
00:16:25,297 --> 00:16:26,798
- The Admiral said
this structure was

403
00:16:26,799 --> 00:16:28,133
on the verge of collapse.

404
00:16:28,134 --> 00:16:29,801
- It could cave in
any minute now.

405
00:16:29,802 --> 00:16:31,469
- Look, is there any way
to send a signal?

406
00:16:31,470 --> 00:16:33,555
- Comms are in
another corridor.

407
00:16:33,556 --> 00:16:34,889
They're too damaged.

408
00:16:34,890 --> 00:16:36,474
And I don't know how to repair

409
00:16:36,475 --> 00:16:37,934
our communications array
anyway.

410
00:16:37,935 --> 00:16:39,811
- Yeah, it's a good job I do.

411
00:16:39,812 --> 00:16:41,354
Listen, you get me to comms,

412
00:16:41,355 --> 00:16:43,857
I should be able to fix it up
well enough to send a signal.

413
00:16:43,858 --> 00:16:45,066
- No.

414
00:16:45,067 --> 00:16:46,985
To get to it,
we have to pass them.

415
00:16:46,986 --> 00:16:48,153
- The creatures?

416
00:16:48,154 --> 00:16:49,654
- No, the raiders.

417
00:16:49,655 --> 00:16:51,531
[metallic rumbling]

418
00:16:51,532 --> 00:16:53,616
- Wait, I'm sorry.
The raiders?

419
00:16:53,617 --> 00:16:55,326
- Yeah, nasty bunch.

420
00:16:55,327 --> 00:16:58,538
I've decided
I really don't like them.

421
00:16:58,539 --> 00:17:02,208
OK, if we want to go
to the comms, now's our chance.

422
00:17:02,209 --> 00:17:05,337
[tense music]

423
00:17:05,338 --> 00:17:08,590
♪ ♪

424
00:17:08,591 --> 00:17:10,050
- Checking corridor D.

425
00:17:10,051 --> 00:17:11,676
- They're here.

426
00:17:11,677 --> 00:17:13,428
[gasps]
- [shushes]

427
00:17:13,429 --> 00:17:20,436
♪ ♪

428
00:17:41,624 --> 00:17:43,708
Who the hell are these guys?

429
00:17:43,709 --> 00:17:44,709
[yelps]

430
00:17:44,710 --> 00:17:51,717
♪ ♪

431
00:17:56,722 --> 00:17:59,432
- Let me ask you something.

432
00:17:59,433 --> 00:18:01,643
Do you think Alicia
would think I killed you

433
00:18:01,644 --> 00:18:03,812
and give up the search?

434
00:18:03,813 --> 00:18:05,647
- I mean, she did
have to stop you

435
00:18:05,648 --> 00:18:09,234
from killing me that time
in the bio shelter.

436
00:18:09,235 --> 00:18:10,610
- Yeah, to this day,

437
00:18:10,611 --> 00:18:12,821
I still don't know
why I messed that up.

438
00:18:12,822 --> 00:18:14,407
Ruined my perfect streak.

439
00:18:18,786 --> 00:18:20,912
[laughs]

440
00:18:20,913 --> 00:18:23,164
- You've got one
dark sense of humor.

441
00:18:23,165 --> 00:18:25,334
- What other kind
would you expect me to have?

442
00:18:27,420 --> 00:18:28,920
- Anyway...

443
00:18:28,921 --> 00:18:30,171
to answer your question,

444
00:18:30,172 --> 00:18:33,883
I think Alicia sees you
as a friend now.

445
00:18:33,884 --> 00:18:35,385
Like I do.

446
00:18:35,386 --> 00:18:37,429
- [chuckles]
You see me as a friend?

447
00:18:37,430 --> 00:18:39,472
- Yeah.

448
00:18:39,473 --> 00:18:41,016
Yeah.

449
00:18:41,017 --> 00:18:42,600
I mean, let's face it.

450
00:18:42,601 --> 00:18:44,269
You're...

451
00:18:44,270 --> 00:18:45,603
not the same person you were

452
00:18:45,604 --> 00:18:47,356
when your mother was
controlling you.

453
00:18:50,484 --> 00:18:52,445
[stone scraping]

454
00:18:56,782 --> 00:18:58,074
What are you doing?

455
00:18:58,075 --> 00:19:00,285
[suspenseful music]

456
00:19:00,286 --> 00:19:02,620
- I think I know
how we can get out.

457
00:19:02,621 --> 00:19:05,915
♪ ♪

458
00:19:05,916 --> 00:19:07,751
Come on, we can crawl through.

459
00:19:07,752 --> 00:19:10,003
- [grunts and pants]

460
00:19:10,004 --> 00:19:11,213
No!

461
00:19:12,381 --> 00:19:15,468
[metallic creaking]

462
00:19:17,345 --> 00:19:19,429
[birds singing]

463
00:19:19,430 --> 00:19:22,140
- If there's a signal
from Angus or Kelly's WDs,

464
00:19:22,141 --> 00:19:23,808
this should amplify it
and allow us to trace it

465
00:19:23,809 --> 00:19:26,644
to its origin point.

466
00:19:26,645 --> 00:19:28,563
It's picking up something,
but it's too faint.

467
00:19:28,564 --> 00:19:30,398
- Is there any way
you can turn it up?

468
00:19:30,399 --> 00:19:33,318
- It's already close
to max power, but I'll try.

469
00:19:33,319 --> 00:19:35,528
I don't want to blow the coil.

470
00:19:35,529 --> 00:19:37,197
[device chiming]
Oh, I've got him.

471
00:19:37,198 --> 00:19:39,115
- Can you get a direction?

472
00:19:39,116 --> 00:19:41,326
- No. The reading's still
not strong enough.

473
00:19:41,327 --> 00:19:42,535
- Turn it up some more.

474
00:19:42,536 --> 00:19:43,620
- I'm afraid
it'll blow the coil.

475
00:19:43,621 --> 00:19:44,996
- Is there something else
you can do?

476
00:19:44,997 --> 00:19:47,207
If we can't get a direction,
the signal does us no good.

477
00:19:47,208 --> 00:19:48,958
- If it blows out,
we may never find them.

478
00:19:48,959 --> 00:19:50,835
- [sighs] If we can't get
a direction for the signal,

479
00:19:50,836 --> 00:19:53,797
we may also never find them.

480
00:19:53,798 --> 00:19:56,092
- OK.

481
00:19:57,134 --> 00:19:58,302
[groans]

482
00:19:59,762 --> 00:20:00,763
[sighs]

483
00:20:02,682 --> 00:20:04,265
[WD chimes]

484
00:20:04,266 --> 00:20:06,226
- Strickland to Garnet.

485
00:20:06,227 --> 00:20:07,310
- Go for Garnet.

486
00:20:07,311 --> 00:20:09,437
- The phased array didn't work.

487
00:20:09,438 --> 00:20:10,647
We searched everywhere
in the zone.

488
00:20:10,648 --> 00:20:13,109
I don't know what else to do.

489
00:20:14,276 --> 00:20:16,778
- I might have an idea.

490
00:20:16,779 --> 00:20:19,197
John Doe seems to be
pretty mobile now.

491
00:20:19,198 --> 00:20:20,615
Kabir got him up to go
to the bathroom.

492
00:20:20,616 --> 00:20:21,574
He can walk.

493
00:20:21,575 --> 00:20:23,159
- I don't follow.

494
00:20:23,160 --> 00:20:25,620
- Well, he's wearing
Angus's clothing,

495
00:20:25,621 --> 00:20:28,540
but you already checked
Angus's cabin, right?

496
00:20:28,541 --> 00:20:30,875
- Yeah, no signs of foul play.

497
00:20:30,876 --> 00:20:34,003
- So maybe he got the clothing
right off Angus's back.

498
00:20:34,004 --> 00:20:36,965
[tense music]

499
00:20:36,966 --> 00:20:41,721
♪ ♪

500
00:20:43,055 --> 00:20:46,474
- Lieutenant Ilic.

501
00:20:46,475 --> 00:20:49,936
- That is my last name,
but I wasn't military.

502
00:20:49,937 --> 00:20:51,146
- Oh.

503
00:20:51,147 --> 00:20:53,565
- How have been
settling in, Admiral?

504
00:20:53,566 --> 00:20:55,483
- Uh...

505
00:20:55,484 --> 00:20:58,403
yeah, what's--
what's not to like?

506
00:20:58,404 --> 00:21:00,488
[chuckling]
Right?

507
00:21:00,489 --> 00:21:02,907
- Well, I heard you caused
quite a stir

508
00:21:02,908 --> 00:21:04,451
taking over command of "Ark 1."

509
00:21:04,452 --> 00:21:07,328
- What can I say?
I like to shake things up.

510
00:21:07,329 --> 00:21:08,788
- I expect you'll be wanting

511
00:21:08,789 --> 00:21:11,041
to shake things up here
as well?

512
00:21:11,042 --> 00:21:14,419
- Well, I have
a couple of ideas.

513
00:21:14,420 --> 00:21:17,338
- Maybe I can show you
around Homebase.

514
00:21:17,339 --> 00:21:20,675
- I'd like that.

515
00:21:20,676 --> 00:21:24,220
I could have sworn we've had
some overlap at GSA command.

516
00:21:24,221 --> 00:21:25,513
- No.

517
00:21:25,514 --> 00:21:28,099
Like I said, I wasn't military.

518
00:21:28,100 --> 00:21:31,102
- I mean, you have a face
that would be hard to forget.

519
00:21:31,103 --> 00:21:34,773
- Oh, I'll take that
as a compliment.

520
00:21:34,774 --> 00:21:36,691
- It is a compliment.

521
00:21:36,692 --> 00:21:41,780
But it must mean you have
a clone at GSA command.

522
00:21:41,781 --> 00:21:44,157
- There are no clones of me,
as far as I know.

523
00:21:44,158 --> 00:21:45,867
[WD chimes]

524
00:21:45,868 --> 00:21:47,744
Mm. I'm sorry.

525
00:21:47,745 --> 00:21:51,164
You'll have to excuse me.
I have a meeting to get to.

526
00:21:51,165 --> 00:21:53,709
- Got to do what we have to do.
- [chuckles softly]

527
00:21:55,586 --> 00:21:58,713
[suspenseful music]

528
00:21:58,714 --> 00:22:01,384
♪ ♪

529
00:22:26,367 --> 00:22:29,327
[ominous music]

530
00:22:29,328 --> 00:22:36,502
♪ ♪

531
00:23:17,251 --> 00:23:18,293
- He's coming your way.

532
00:23:18,294 --> 00:23:21,629
♪ ♪

533
00:23:21,630 --> 00:23:25,341
- Got him on the tracker.

534
00:23:25,342 --> 00:23:26,802
- [groans softly]

535
00:23:30,973 --> 00:23:33,809
[indistinct chatter]

536
00:23:37,063 --> 00:23:38,855
- He's leaving the safe zone.

537
00:23:38,856 --> 00:23:40,273
- Do we follow?

538
00:23:40,274 --> 00:23:44,569
- Eva said we have 5K buffer
before the air gets bad.

539
00:23:44,570 --> 00:23:48,323
- Breadcrumb it and keep track
of how far we go.

540
00:23:48,324 --> 00:23:50,033
- Copy.

541
00:23:50,034 --> 00:23:52,994
[tense music]

542
00:23:52,995 --> 00:24:00,169
♪ ♪

543
00:24:08,636 --> 00:24:11,596
[soft suspenseful music]

544
00:24:11,597 --> 00:24:16,726
♪ ♪

545
00:24:16,727 --> 00:24:17,977
Well, that's new.

546
00:24:17,978 --> 00:24:20,021
- What is that?

547
00:24:20,022 --> 00:24:24,275
♪ ♪

548
00:24:24,276 --> 00:24:25,735
[rumbling]

549
00:24:25,736 --> 00:24:28,905
[tense music]

550
00:24:28,906 --> 00:24:36,038
♪ ♪

551
00:24:48,509 --> 00:24:49,676
- What the hell was that?

552
00:24:49,677 --> 00:24:51,052
- I don't know.

553
00:24:51,053 --> 00:24:52,470
But I'm going after him.

554
00:24:52,471 --> 00:24:53,638
Cover me.

555
00:24:53,639 --> 00:25:00,479
♪ ♪

556
00:25:10,239 --> 00:25:13,199
There's nothing here.

557
00:25:13,200 --> 00:25:15,535
- What if we touch the stone
like he did?

558
00:25:15,536 --> 00:25:18,496
[soft suspenseful music]

559
00:25:18,497 --> 00:25:20,415
♪ ♪

560
00:25:20,416 --> 00:25:21,458
Anything?

561
00:25:21,459 --> 00:25:22,917
♪ ♪

562
00:25:22,918 --> 00:25:24,794
- Strickland to Garnet.

563
00:25:24,795 --> 00:25:26,087
- Go for Garnet.

564
00:25:26,088 --> 00:25:28,006
- We found where he must
have been hiding out,

565
00:25:28,007 --> 00:25:29,132
but we can't get in.

566
00:25:29,133 --> 00:25:30,508
- What do you mean?

567
00:25:30,509 --> 00:25:33,511
- The entrance is
camouflaged somehow.

568
00:25:33,512 --> 00:25:35,346
- You're gonna want to see this
for yourself, Captain.

569
00:25:35,347 --> 00:25:37,348
- Send me the coordinates.
I'll meet you there.

570
00:25:37,349 --> 00:25:44,272
♪ ♪

571
00:25:44,273 --> 00:25:45,690
- OK.

572
00:25:45,691 --> 00:25:47,359
- [sighs]

573
00:25:53,074 --> 00:25:54,657
Wipe it down with
those antiseptic wipes.

574
00:25:54,658 --> 00:25:56,619
- Yep.

575
00:26:04,752 --> 00:26:05,794
- OK.

576
00:26:05,795 --> 00:26:07,671
- OK, here.

577
00:26:07,672 --> 00:26:08,880
Take this.

578
00:26:08,881 --> 00:26:11,926
You have to fix me.

579
00:26:13,969 --> 00:26:17,806
- Like, um...

580
00:26:17,807 --> 00:26:19,683
I don't want to hurt you.

581
00:26:19,684 --> 00:26:23,603
- Just sew it up like
you're sewing up a seam.

582
00:26:23,604 --> 00:26:26,147
- Right.

583
00:26:26,148 --> 00:26:27,316
[clears throat]

584
00:26:28,943 --> 00:26:31,861
[soft suspenseful music]

585
00:26:31,862 --> 00:26:34,030
♪ ♪

586
00:26:34,031 --> 00:26:36,533
- [groaning softly]

587
00:26:36,534 --> 00:26:39,661
- Ah--sorry, sorry.

588
00:26:39,662 --> 00:26:40,954
[sighs]

589
00:26:40,955 --> 00:26:42,705
[soft dramatic music]

590
00:26:42,706 --> 00:26:47,043
So, uh, what made you want
to become a medic, anyway?

591
00:26:47,044 --> 00:26:48,378
- [chuckles softly]

592
00:26:48,379 --> 00:26:53,591
The truth is I've always
been kind of a caretaker.

593
00:26:53,592 --> 00:26:55,719
It's like...

594
00:26:55,720 --> 00:26:58,763
it's what I was made to do.

595
00:26:58,764 --> 00:27:00,849
[gasps]
- Sorry, sorry.

596
00:27:00,850 --> 00:27:04,644
I think clearly, it is not
what I was made to do.

597
00:27:04,645 --> 00:27:07,522
[both chuckle]

598
00:27:07,523 --> 00:27:11,110
♪ ♪

599
00:27:21,662 --> 00:27:22,829
- Ooh.

600
00:27:22,830 --> 00:27:24,748
[clicks tongue]

601
00:27:24,749 --> 00:27:27,709
Bad day.

602
00:27:27,710 --> 00:27:31,212
I had a feeling
I might find you here.

603
00:27:31,213 --> 00:27:35,216
- I'm flattered you find me
so interesting, Admiral.

604
00:27:35,217 --> 00:27:38,053
- You are very intriguing.

605
00:27:38,054 --> 00:27:39,596
Lieutenant Commander.

606
00:27:39,597 --> 00:27:41,306
[tense music]

607
00:27:41,307 --> 00:27:44,642
- I told you I'm not military.

608
00:27:44,643 --> 00:27:45,852
- Really?

609
00:27:45,853 --> 00:27:49,522
That's not what our
military records say.

610
00:27:49,523 --> 00:27:53,735
And the strange thing is
there are a lot of redactions.

611
00:27:53,736 --> 00:27:58,740
Huge swaths
of blacked-out information.

612
00:27:58,741 --> 00:28:00,742
[sighs]

613
00:28:00,743 --> 00:28:02,744
So tell me.

614
00:28:02,745 --> 00:28:05,330
Who are you?

615
00:28:05,331 --> 00:28:07,165
- You ask a lot of questions.

616
00:28:07,166 --> 00:28:09,000
- Just trying
to get to know you.

617
00:28:09,001 --> 00:28:13,088
♪ ♪

618
00:28:13,089 --> 00:28:17,050
- If I were you,
I'd lose interest.

619
00:28:17,051 --> 00:28:20,011
[tense music]

620
00:28:20,012 --> 00:28:27,186
♪ ♪

621
00:28:29,980 --> 00:28:31,815
- Wow.

622
00:28:31,816 --> 00:28:33,858
Who could have built this?

623
00:28:33,859 --> 00:28:35,860
- Amazing, isn't it?

624
00:28:35,861 --> 00:28:38,113
- Our man opened the hatch
with this thing and went in,

625
00:28:38,114 --> 00:28:40,699
which means Angus and Kelly
could be inside.

626
00:28:40,700 --> 00:28:42,200
- A hatch?

627
00:28:42,201 --> 00:28:44,244
- He touched the stone
in some sort of pattern.

628
00:28:44,245 --> 00:28:46,247
- Like, uh, this.

629
00:28:48,666 --> 00:28:50,250
- And a hatch opened
over there,

630
00:28:50,251 --> 00:28:52,210
but we can't seem
to reproduce what he did.

631
00:28:52,211 --> 00:28:54,796
There aren't any buttons
or anything.

632
00:28:54,797 --> 00:28:57,465
- Is it possible he's
the only one that can open it?

633
00:28:57,466 --> 00:29:00,136
Maybe there's something
special about him.

634
00:29:01,804 --> 00:29:03,763
- He does have
different ears from us.

635
00:29:03,764 --> 00:29:06,600
Could the mechanism have
something to do with sound?

636
00:29:09,020 --> 00:29:10,562
- Maybe this door is activated

637
00:29:10,563 --> 00:29:12,564
by something outside
of the range of our hearing.

638
00:29:12,565 --> 00:29:14,858
I might be able to use
my WD to look for a sound

639
00:29:14,859 --> 00:29:18,653
and transpose the frequency
into a range we can hear.

640
00:29:18,654 --> 00:29:20,447
[WD buzzing]

641
00:29:20,448 --> 00:29:22,323
[musical notes warbling]
There.

642
00:29:22,324 --> 00:29:23,742
Those notes are
coming from the stone.

643
00:29:23,743 --> 00:29:26,828
My WD is adjusting it
into our hearing range.

644
00:29:26,829 --> 00:29:29,664
[musical notes warbling]
- Sounds like a musical scale.

645
00:29:29,665 --> 00:29:34,169
- It's a C major scale,
but it's missing three notes.

646
00:29:34,170 --> 00:29:36,254
[musical notes warbling]

647
00:29:36,255 --> 00:29:37,756
I used to play piano.

648
00:29:37,757 --> 00:29:40,050
- Really?

649
00:29:40,051 --> 00:29:41,676
- What if you touched the stone
in the same way he did,

650
00:29:41,677 --> 00:29:43,887
but only when you hear
a gap in the scale?

651
00:29:43,888 --> 00:29:45,972
- OK.

652
00:29:45,973 --> 00:29:48,933
[musical notes warbling]

653
00:29:48,934 --> 00:29:50,852
♪ ♪

654
00:29:50,853 --> 00:29:52,937
[musical notes warbling]

655
00:29:52,938 --> 00:29:55,691
[rumbling]

656
00:29:58,402 --> 00:30:00,362
- That is so cool.

657
00:30:00,363 --> 00:30:03,365
[suspenseful music]

658
00:30:03,366 --> 00:30:10,664
♪ ♪

659
00:30:11,415 --> 00:30:13,541
You really think
they're down there?

660
00:30:13,542 --> 00:30:15,043
- Only one way to find out.

661
00:30:15,044 --> 00:30:16,711
- Flashlights out.

662
00:30:16,712 --> 00:30:21,841
♪ ♪

663
00:30:21,842 --> 00:30:23,176
- Stay alert.

664
00:30:23,177 --> 00:30:24,969
Who knows what's waiting
for us down there?

665
00:30:24,970 --> 00:30:27,722
And alert Homebase
that we're going in.

666
00:30:27,723 --> 00:30:29,725
♪ ♪

667
00:30:34,063 --> 00:30:36,815
[suspenseful music]

668
00:30:36,816 --> 00:30:41,653
♪ ♪

669
00:30:41,654 --> 00:30:43,738
- Captain, look.

670
00:30:43,739 --> 00:30:47,701
♪ ♪

671
00:30:47,702 --> 00:30:49,327
- That's just like
the slab Angus found.

672
00:30:49,328 --> 00:30:54,749
♪ ♪

673
00:30:54,750 --> 00:30:56,334
- This can't be a natural cave.

674
00:30:56,335 --> 00:30:59,254
It must've been built
by someone.

675
00:30:59,255 --> 00:31:01,715
- Yeah, but who?

676
00:31:01,716 --> 00:31:03,174
Or what?

677
00:31:03,175 --> 00:31:10,099
♪ ♪

678
00:31:12,768 --> 00:31:13,810
- What was that?

679
00:31:13,811 --> 00:31:15,228
- I don't know.

680
00:31:15,229 --> 00:31:17,355
- Stay close.

681
00:31:17,356 --> 00:31:24,321
♪ ♪

682
00:31:40,212 --> 00:31:42,338
- So this is where
you've been sleeping.

683
00:31:42,339 --> 00:31:45,091
- [groans]

684
00:31:45,092 --> 00:31:46,968
Yeah.

685
00:31:46,969 --> 00:31:49,095
Didn't feel comfortable
in our cabin.

686
00:31:49,096 --> 00:31:50,138
- I--

687
00:31:50,139 --> 00:31:52,557
I never said
you couldn't sleep there.

688
00:31:52,558 --> 00:31:55,727
- [sighs]

689
00:31:55,728 --> 00:31:58,188
I know.

690
00:31:58,189 --> 00:31:59,814
But I know
you were angry with me

691
00:31:59,815 --> 00:32:03,568
because of the way
I reacted to...

692
00:32:03,569 --> 00:32:05,196
- Yeah.
- Yeah.

693
00:32:08,908 --> 00:32:10,617
Hey, Sanji.

694
00:32:10,618 --> 00:32:11,701
- Mm?

695
00:32:11,702 --> 00:32:13,953
- I'm really sorry.

696
00:32:13,954 --> 00:32:17,665
It's just that guy,
he gets under my skin.

697
00:32:17,666 --> 00:32:19,043
- Yeah.

698
00:32:21,045 --> 00:32:23,214
How do you think I feel?

699
00:32:25,758 --> 00:32:28,010
- [sighs]

700
00:32:30,638 --> 00:32:34,392
- She's a piece of work,
isn't she?

701
00:32:38,104 --> 00:32:40,231
- [sighs]

702
00:32:43,192 --> 00:32:45,693
- I know you don't like me.

703
00:32:45,694 --> 00:32:46,861
- Oh, yeah?

704
00:32:46,862 --> 00:32:49,572
- You got it all wrong.

705
00:32:49,573 --> 00:32:53,910
It's not all my fault.

706
00:32:53,911 --> 00:32:59,249
Sanji can be
a real bitch sometimes.

707
00:32:59,250 --> 00:33:02,335
[coughing]

708
00:33:02,336 --> 00:33:05,380
[tense music]

709
00:33:05,381 --> 00:33:10,552
♪ ♪

710
00:33:10,553 --> 00:33:12,637
- Which way do we go?

711
00:33:12,638 --> 00:33:19,352
♪ ♪

712
00:33:19,353 --> 00:33:22,188
- Heavier foot traffic
in the tunnel to the right.

713
00:33:22,189 --> 00:33:23,481
- So let's go that way.

714
00:33:23,482 --> 00:33:28,194
♪ ♪

715
00:33:28,195 --> 00:33:32,741
- Maybe they won't feed us
anymore after what I did.

716
00:33:32,742 --> 00:33:34,826
- They want us alive.

717
00:33:34,827 --> 00:33:37,412
- OK. I just--
I think that they might--

718
00:33:37,413 --> 00:33:38,371
[stone scraping]

719
00:33:38,372 --> 00:33:41,416
[dramatic music]

720
00:33:41,417 --> 00:33:48,340
♪ ♪

721
00:33:56,599 --> 00:33:57,682
She's--
- Human.

722
00:33:57,683 --> 00:33:59,559
- [harmonic screaming]

723
00:33:59,560 --> 00:34:01,019
- Let her go!

724
00:34:01,020 --> 00:34:02,854
- Just slide under
the door first.

725
00:34:02,855 --> 00:34:04,105
- You saw what happened
to that tray!

726
00:34:04,106 --> 00:34:05,940
- They won't do that
to one of their own.

727
00:34:05,941 --> 00:34:07,442
- [harmonic screaming]

728
00:34:07,443 --> 00:34:09,819
- OK, but if I end up
flattened, that's on you!

729
00:34:09,820 --> 00:34:12,781
- [harmonic screaming]

730
00:34:12,782 --> 00:34:14,783
♪ ♪

731
00:34:14,784 --> 00:34:17,535
- [gasps]

732
00:34:17,536 --> 00:34:20,497
- [harmonic screaming]

733
00:34:20,498 --> 00:34:23,541
[tense music]

734
00:34:23,542 --> 00:34:26,336
♪ ♪

735
00:34:26,337 --> 00:34:28,630
- Uh, Angus?

736
00:34:28,631 --> 00:34:30,423
Who are they?

737
00:34:30,424 --> 00:34:32,967
- I got no idea.

738
00:34:32,968 --> 00:34:35,053
- That one's
wearing your clothes.

739
00:34:35,054 --> 00:34:40,475
- Please, please,
just tell us what you want.

740
00:34:40,476 --> 00:34:41,559
- Get back!

741
00:34:41,560 --> 00:34:44,896
♪ ♪

742
00:34:44,897 --> 00:34:47,816
[soft suspenseful music]

743
00:34:47,817 --> 00:34:51,986
♪ ♪

744
00:34:51,987 --> 00:34:54,823
[WD chiming]

745
00:34:54,824 --> 00:34:55,907
♪ ♪

746
00:34:55,908 --> 00:34:58,284
- It's Kelly and Angus's WDs.

747
00:34:58,285 --> 00:35:00,328
We're getting a signal.

748
00:35:00,329 --> 00:35:02,414
This way.

749
00:35:02,415 --> 00:35:03,832
- Don't worry.
I have my implants turned on.

750
00:35:03,833 --> 00:35:04,874
- No, Kelly.

751
00:35:04,875 --> 00:35:05,917
Kelly, don't hurt them unless

752
00:35:05,918 --> 00:35:07,210
it's obvious
they're gonna hurt us.

753
00:35:07,211 --> 00:35:09,504
- I'm not letting them
put us back in that room.

754
00:35:09,505 --> 00:35:11,756
- Hey, hey, please.
We don't want to hurt you.

755
00:35:11,757 --> 00:35:15,635
Listen, tell us what you want.
What do you want from us?

756
00:35:15,636 --> 00:35:17,804
[tense music]

757
00:35:17,805 --> 00:35:20,724
[all screaming]

758
00:35:20,725 --> 00:35:21,975
What's happening?

759
00:35:21,976 --> 00:35:24,853
[all screaming]

760
00:35:24,854 --> 00:35:26,980
[high-pitched ringing playing]

761
00:35:26,981 --> 00:35:28,356
- [gasps] Angus!

762
00:35:28,357 --> 00:35:30,483
[soft dramatic music]

763
00:35:30,484 --> 00:35:31,568
Angus.

764
00:35:31,569 --> 00:35:32,861
- I don't understand.

765
00:35:32,862 --> 00:35:34,904
What--what chased them off?

766
00:35:34,905 --> 00:35:36,906
- I-I used my WD
to duplicate a frequency

767
00:35:36,907 --> 00:35:37,991
that hurts their ears.

768
00:35:37,992 --> 00:35:39,409
- How'd you know
how to do that?

769
00:35:39,410 --> 00:35:41,161
- Come on.

770
00:35:41,162 --> 00:35:44,164
Let's save it
for when we get out of here.

771
00:35:44,165 --> 00:35:46,292
[dramatic music]

772
00:35:48,711 --> 00:35:51,505
[electrical thumping]

773
00:35:54,425 --> 00:35:56,134
- [sighs] Looks good.

774
00:35:56,135 --> 00:35:58,303
Power is restored.

775
00:35:58,304 --> 00:35:59,471
- Mm, yep.

776
00:35:59,472 --> 00:36:01,181
Nobody will notice
the difference.

777
00:36:01,182 --> 00:36:04,142
- Yeah, except the people
in those caves.

778
00:36:04,143 --> 00:36:07,979
- I hope it wasn't a mistake
lowering the VCN frequency.

779
00:36:07,980 --> 00:36:10,648
- Well, Sharon thought it might
be a good peace offering

780
00:36:10,649 --> 00:36:14,694
to, you know,
stop hurting the locals' ears.

781
00:36:14,695 --> 00:36:16,946
- Except it could have been
all that was keeping them

782
00:36:16,947 --> 00:36:18,031
from attacking us.

783
00:36:18,032 --> 00:36:21,076
[tense music]

784
00:36:21,077 --> 00:36:22,118
- mm.

785
00:36:22,119 --> 00:36:24,454
♪ ♪

786
00:36:24,455 --> 00:36:26,664
- You did some serious damage.

787
00:36:26,665 --> 00:36:29,334
- I told her to stop
hitting the wall.

788
00:36:29,335 --> 00:36:31,836
- Your nanites should take care
of it fairly quickly,

789
00:36:31,837 --> 00:36:33,046
but you should keep it clean.

790
00:36:33,047 --> 00:36:34,547
- Uh, can you stay still?

791
00:36:34,548 --> 00:36:35,548
I'm trying to take
your blood pressure.

792
00:36:35,549 --> 00:36:37,300
- Sorry.
- Is he OK?

793
00:36:37,301 --> 00:36:38,927
- Far as I can tell, he's fine.

794
00:36:38,928 --> 00:36:40,136
- We're glad you're both safe,

795
00:36:40,137 --> 00:36:41,971
but we need to ask you
a few questions

796
00:36:41,972 --> 00:36:43,723
about what you saw down there.

797
00:36:43,724 --> 00:36:46,226
- How many people did you see
before Garnet arrived?

798
00:36:46,227 --> 00:36:47,602
- Were they violent toward you?

799
00:36:47,603 --> 00:36:48,937
- They were, uh--

800
00:36:48,938 --> 00:36:50,647
- It's also important
we understand

801
00:36:50,648 --> 00:36:52,690
what kinds of power sources
they have.

802
00:36:52,691 --> 00:36:54,484
- Were they armed?
- They need to rest.

803
00:36:54,485 --> 00:36:55,735
- This can't wait.

804
00:36:55,736 --> 00:36:57,445
We need to know
what we're dealing with.

805
00:36:57,446 --> 00:36:59,156
- It can wait until tomorrow.

806
00:37:03,077 --> 00:37:04,452
- All right.

807
00:37:04,453 --> 00:37:05,995
Until tomorrow.

808
00:37:05,996 --> 00:37:08,081
- Thank you.

809
00:37:08,082 --> 00:37:10,417
Rest up.

810
00:37:10,418 --> 00:37:12,502
- Um--sorry, one second.

811
00:37:12,503 --> 00:37:14,212
Can I talk to you for a minute?

812
00:37:14,213 --> 00:37:15,505
- I don't have time.

813
00:37:15,506 --> 00:37:17,091
- Sharon, just hear me out.

814
00:37:20,136 --> 00:37:23,555
Once Dr. Hall died,
I should have told you.

815
00:37:23,556 --> 00:37:25,098
- Yes, you should have.

816
00:37:25,099 --> 00:37:27,892
- But you were so worried about
ending up like your sister,

817
00:37:27,893 --> 00:37:30,061
I didn't want
to exacerbate that worry

818
00:37:30,062 --> 00:37:32,230
by telling you
you were Gen-3.

819
00:37:32,231 --> 00:37:35,275
- That was not
your decision to make.

820
00:37:35,276 --> 00:37:39,112
- You're right. I'm sorry.

821
00:37:39,113 --> 00:37:43,658
You said Drake had a room
full of Gen-3s on his ship?

822
00:37:43,659 --> 00:37:46,411
- At least one.

823
00:37:46,412 --> 00:37:47,787
- Then who knows
how many more Gen-3s

824
00:37:47,788 --> 00:37:48,955
could be out there, right?

825
00:37:48,956 --> 00:37:51,541
I mean, Drake's company made
a lot of clones.

826
00:37:51,542 --> 00:37:53,293
- Is there a point?

827
00:37:53,294 --> 00:37:54,919
- Yes.

828
00:37:54,920 --> 00:37:57,881
Drake thought he could find
the answer in you,

829
00:37:57,882 --> 00:38:02,510
but he didn't have
one thing I do.

830
00:38:02,511 --> 00:38:06,097
Dr. Hall's research.

831
00:38:06,098 --> 00:38:09,768
- OK, but you said
he couldn't reproduce the cure.

832
00:38:09,769 --> 00:38:13,897
- Well, he might have
had he lived long enough.

833
00:38:13,898 --> 00:38:15,315
Let's finish his work.

834
00:38:15,316 --> 00:38:16,900
[soft dramatic music]

835
00:38:16,901 --> 00:38:20,487
- Uh, so you want
to experiment on me?

836
00:38:20,488 --> 00:38:25,450
- No.
I-I want to find a cure.

837
00:38:25,451 --> 00:38:28,453
Together.

838
00:38:28,454 --> 00:38:33,124
♪ ♪

839
00:38:33,125 --> 00:38:34,459
- I would like that.

840
00:38:34,460 --> 00:38:37,629
- [sighs]
Really?

841
00:38:37,630 --> 00:38:39,881
- Come here.
Bring it in.

842
00:38:39,882 --> 00:38:44,135
Jerk. I really wanted
to stay mad at you.

843
00:38:44,136 --> 00:38:47,472
- I'm sorry.

844
00:38:47,473 --> 00:38:49,099
- Me too.

845
00:38:49,100 --> 00:38:52,103
♪ ♪

846
00:39:01,821 --> 00:39:03,364
- Can I help you
with something, Admiral?

847
00:39:09,662 --> 00:39:11,121
- Cut the BS.

848
00:39:11,122 --> 00:39:12,706
- I'm sorry?

849
00:39:12,707 --> 00:39:13,915
- Now, I was
pleasantly surprised

850
00:39:13,916 --> 00:39:15,291
to see that
my Admiral clearance code

851
00:39:15,292 --> 00:39:18,003
still gave me access
to redacted files.

852
00:39:18,004 --> 00:39:19,296
I got to read all about you.

853
00:39:25,177 --> 00:39:27,722
I always thought
Black Thorn was a rumor.

854
00:39:34,437 --> 00:39:38,732
Tell me, do your cohorts on
the council know about this?

855
00:39:38,733 --> 00:39:41,568
[soft suspenseful music]

856
00:39:41,569 --> 00:39:45,196
- You know...

857
00:39:45,197 --> 00:39:48,826
I really wished
you let this go.

858
00:39:49,618 --> 00:39:50,910
- [groans]

859
00:39:50,911 --> 00:39:53,830
[tense music]

860
00:39:53,831 --> 00:39:58,918
♪ ♪

861
00:39:58,919 --> 00:40:01,087
[somber music]

862
00:40:01,088 --> 00:40:02,881
[groans]

863
00:40:02,882 --> 00:40:10,014
♪ ♪

864
00:40:14,060 --> 00:40:15,602
- Shit.

865
00:40:15,603 --> 00:40:18,563
[tense music]

866
00:40:18,564 --> 00:40:21,691
♪ ♪

867
00:40:21,692 --> 00:40:23,068
- I'm close.

868
00:40:23,069 --> 00:40:24,527
Just need to adjust
the frequency

869
00:40:24,528 --> 00:40:26,237
to get the signal out.

870
00:40:26,238 --> 00:40:28,865
♪ ♪

871
00:40:28,866 --> 00:40:30,617
- Someone's coming.

872
00:40:30,618 --> 00:40:32,243
Keep going.

873
00:40:32,244 --> 00:40:33,578
- Rebooting now.

874
00:40:33,579 --> 00:40:35,497
- [gasps]

875
00:40:35,498 --> 00:40:36,581
♪ ♪

876
00:40:36,582 --> 00:40:38,917
- Come on, come on.

877
00:40:38,918 --> 00:40:40,294
- Keep going!

878
00:40:41,003 --> 00:40:42,379
[computer chimes]
- Yes! It sent!

879
00:40:42,380 --> 00:40:44,756
[gunshots]

880
00:40:44,757 --> 00:40:46,591
♪ ♪

881
00:40:46,592 --> 00:40:48,426
[screams]

882
00:40:48,427 --> 00:40:51,179
[tense somber music]

883
00:40:51,180 --> 00:40:53,765
No, no, no.
Hey, hey, hey. Hey.

884
00:40:53,766 --> 00:40:55,100
Stay with me, Doc.

885
00:40:55,101 --> 00:40:57,435
OK?

886
00:40:57,436 --> 00:41:01,648
- You have to fix me.

887
00:41:01,649 --> 00:41:02,690
Buddy.

888
00:41:02,691 --> 00:41:07,487
♪ ♪

889
00:41:07,488 --> 00:41:08,697
- No.

890
00:41:08,698 --> 00:41:15,788
♪ ♪

891
00:41:17,665 --> 00:41:21,793
No, no, no, no.

892
00:41:21,794 --> 00:41:24,754
[computerized warbling]

893
00:41:24,755 --> 00:41:29,467
♪ ♪

894
00:41:29,468 --> 00:41:31,553
What the hell?

895
00:41:31,554 --> 00:41:38,519
♪ ♪

896
00:41:39,729 --> 00:41:41,146
[computer chimes]

897
00:41:41,147 --> 00:41:44,274
[suspenseful music]

898
00:41:44,275 --> 00:41:51,282
♪ ♪

899
00:42:00,040 --> 00:42:03,001
[somber music]

900
00:42:03,002 --> 00:42:10,176
♪ ♪

